## B. APP DRAWER / APP LIBRARY

The second and third screenshots demonstrate the main application library.

This interface is particularly important because it defines the identity of the launcher.

Do not replace it with a conventional application grid.

### Visual characteristics

- Same forest wallpaper as the home screen.
- A translucent, soft grey overlay over the wallpaper.
- Subtle frosted-glass appearance.
- Application names arranged vertically.
- Applications organized alphabetically.
- Small monochromatic app icon containers.
- Elegant serif/italic app-label typography.
- Alphabetical index aligned vertically on the right edge.
- Floating circular search control at the bottom-right corner.

### Layout

The application library should use a vertically scrolling alphabetical list.

Example:

A

[icon] Assistant

[icon] Authenticator

[icon] Adobe Scan

[icon] Albums

[icon] Another App


B

[icon] Brave

[icon] BitChord

[icon] Bluetooth

[icon] Banking App


C

[icon] Calendar

[icon] Camera

[icon] Chrome

Continue through the alphabet.

Use actual installed applications from Android. The examples above are only visual illustrations. Never hardcode an artificial application list.

### Application rows

Each row should contain:

- A compact monochrome icon container.
- The appropriate application icon.
- Application name.
- Consistent vertical alignment.
- A generous enough touch area for comfortable interaction.

Visual treatment:
- Dark charcoal or near-black icon container.
- Rounded-square shape.
- Small white icon glyph.
- Optional subtle accent for selected icons.
- Application labels in a refined serif or serif-italic typeface.
- Labels should be readable and truncate gracefully when necessary.

Avoid oversized icons and unnecessary backgrounds around individual rows.

The list should feel light, elegant, and almost editorial.

### Alphabetical organization

The application library must automatically:

1. Retrieve installed launchable applications.
2. Obtain their actual application names.
3. Sort them alphabetically.
4. Group them by their first alphabetical character.
5. Display section headings.
6. Handle applications beginning with numbers or special characters in a separate `#` group.
7. Support different device locales.
8. Update when applications are installed, removed, enabled, or changed.

### Alphabet index

Implement a slim vertical alphabet navigation rail on the right edge.

Requirements:
- A star/favorites entry at the top.
- Letters A–Z.
- Small and unobtrusive typography.
- Vertically centered within the available screen area.
- Semi-transparent appearance.
- Active letter can receive a subtle highlight.
- Tapping any letter should scroll directly to its corresponding section.
- Support quick letter navigation without creating an oversized touch-blocking overlay.
- Handle letters without matching applications gracefully.

The alphabet rail should remain visually subordinate to the application list.

### Search

A floating circular search control should appear near the bottom-right corner.

On tapping:
- Transition into an elegant search state.
- Display a translucent search field.
- Bring up the Android keyboard.
- Allow immediate application filtering.
- Search should be case-insensitive.
- Results must update as the user types.
- Preserve the same visual language as the application library.
- Provide a minimal empty-results state.
- Include an easy way to clear and close the search.

Search should be fast, including on devices with many installed applications.

